\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Takeaway: the registered coverage extension splits its own predictions — the harm replicates strongly for two new judges and is absent for one (P-X1 not confirmed), while the fix's contrast holds for all three (P-X2 confirmed) but its magnitude ranges from near-full removal to a small dent.} Frozen estimators of the mitigation experiment applied unchanged to three judges that had produced no scores at registration time ($n{=}53$ items at $k{>}1$); the prediction ledger records the P-X1 failure. Mistral's null is a score-resolution artifact, not anchoring resistance: it emits exact integers (11 distinct values pool-wide), tying 32 of 64 samples at the maximum against 1 for every other judge, so selection cannot act on its real but mild anchoring (margin $+1.38$).}}{18}{table.caption.34}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:coverage}{{11}{18}{\textbf {Takeaway: the registered coverage extension splits its own predictions — the harm replicates strongly for two new judges and is absent for one (P-X1 not confirmed), while the fix's contrast holds for all three (P-X2 confirmed) but its magnitude ranges from near-full removal to a small dent.} Frozen estimators of the mitigation experiment applied unchanged to three judges that had produced no scores at registration time ($n{=}53$ items at $k{>}1$); the prediction ledger records the P-X1 failure. Mistral's null is a score-resolution artifact, not anchoring resistance: it emits exact integers (11 distinct values pool-wide), tying 32 of 64 samples at the maximum against 1 for every other judge, so selection cannot act on its real but mild anchoring (margin $+1.38$)}{table.caption.34}{}}
